scores.note:
Option A is the only design that measured what assigning arms per lineage costs. I re-read /tmp/lev-design/option-a-session-ab/cluster_mde.out: design effect 7.6-8.6 for D3 and 27-37 for D4. At 2 weeks the unadjusted MDE is 12.02 pp/week for D3 and 12.04 for D4, against expected effects of 3.9-5.1 and 1.4-5.2. So the online verdict depends on two covariates: return_s for D3 and the external wait for D4. The D4 external wait is measured differently in each arm (in treatment it starts at the first denied call), so the treatment itself can move it and bias the estimate. The balance gate turns that into STALL rather than a wrong KEEP. The design sets the smallest improvement acted on equal to the per-half MDE (1.49 vs 1.5, 1.99 vs 2.0). The noise floor therefore sits at that threshold, not below it as the method requires. D1 and D2 are modelled only. Rollback is good:
- a per-lever control file, read on every hook call;
- a corrupt file reads as shadow and raises an alarm;
- env clamps can only lower a lever;
- `cc-lever rollback` is one command;
- auto-revert latches, and re-enabling needs an operator ack;
- treatment shares stay at 0.25-0.5.
Gaps: there is no dead-man switch. The tick heartbeat is checked only at round-start, so if the hourly tick dies mid-round the levers stay enforced. The cadence is hourly, and the tripwires use one-sided 99% bounds, so they are slow to trip. It also edits hf_succ_stage in handoff-fire.sh. Build cost is about 1.0M output tokens over 11 waves (estimated from the design's wave sizes).
